  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nMoisture levels and rain chances will increase Tuesday morning with \nthe approach of a mid-level shortwave trough. Current PWAT values \nare in the 1.3-1.6 inch range as of Monday night but will increase \nto around 1.70 to approaching 2.0 inches by 12Z Tuesday morning. \nIncreasing low-level moisture combined with the approaching \nshortwave trough will enhance convergence and lift, supporting \nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms across the region \n(20-40% across western counties), with higher chances (40-75%) along \neastern counties. Moderate to high will move further inland \nthroughout Tuesday with convergence associated with an inverted \ntrough and abundant moisture. However, rain chances will gradually \ntaper off late Tuesday afternoon and evening as the main forcing \nshifts inland and weakens. \n\nRain chances will continue Wednesday as deep moisture remains in \nplace and additional disturbances move across the region within \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Attention then turns to Thursday as \nanother shortwave and increasing moisture support a renewed round of \nshowers and thunderstorms, with the greatest coverage expected \nacross western portions of South Texas. WPC has placed the Brush \nCountry under a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) of excessive \nrainfall Thursday, with a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 4) extending \ninto northwestern portions of the region. Locally heavy rainfall and \nisolated flash flooding will be possible, especially where storms \nbecome persistent or training develops. Isolated flooding and \nponding will be possible in low lying poor drainage areas. However, \nuncertainty remains regarding the location and magnitude of the \nheaviest rainfall.\n\nRain chances gradually decrease Friday as the deeper moisture and \nupper-level forcing shift northeastward. Low rain chances may linger \ninto Halloween weekend, with another potential cold front \napproaching early next week. Temperatures will remain near to \nslightly above normal through much of the period.\n\nMinor coastal flooding remains a concern through much of the week as \npersistent onshore flow, elevated swell periods, and astronomical \ntides contribute to higher water levels during high tide. An \nincreased risk of rip currents will also persist.",
